 ],
 "literal": "The sun too has set. Listen to the sound! / Do not go, O companion of the small female elephant! / Since a merchant caravan has arrived and camped due to the harshness of the hostile borderland, / holding long spears adorned with metal bands, / coming to the protective forest and patrolling, is the sound of the taṇṇumai drum.",
 "translation": "The sun has set. Listen to that sound! Do not go forward, O companion of the small female elephant! Because a merchant caravan has arrived and camped here to escape the harshness of the hostile borderlands, the guards, holding long spears adorned with metal bands, patrol the protective forest, and the sound of their taṇṇumai drums reverberates.",
 "modern": "சூரியனும் மறைந்துவிட்டது. அந்த ஒலியைக் கேள்! சிறிய பெண் யானையின் துணையே, மேலே செல்லாதே. பகைமை நிறைந்த எல்லையின் கொடுமையால் வணிகர் கூட்டம் இங்கு வந்து தங்கியிருப்பதால், வளையங்கள் அணிந்த நீண்ட வேலேந்தி, காவற்காட்டில் வந்து உலாவிப் பாதுகாக்கும் காவலர்களின் தண்ணுமைப் பறையின் ஒலி அது!",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the arid Pālai landscape, where bystanders observe a young couple eloping through a dangerous desert path. They address their warning to a male elephant, which serves as a poignant metaphor for the young hero accompanying his delicate bride. The bystanders point out that the sun has already set and a loud drumbeat can be heard in the distance. This sound belongs to the taṇṇumai drums of guards who are patrolling the protective forest-border to secure a merchant caravan that has camped there. Since these armed guards carry long, metal-banded spears and patrol actively, the path ahead is highly unsafe. By advising the elephant to halt, the bystanders kindly warn the young lovers to stop their journey for the night and avoid danger.",
